WHAT NULL_POOL MEANS, and why the approach was wrong rather than the run:
removing the HasCode() gate worked -- the caller resolved AND its Code was
obtained. `Code::object_pool()` then returned null, because AOT runs in bare-
instructions mode with ONE GLOBAL pool: object.cc:18451 returns
`IsolateGroup::Current()->object_store()->global_object_pool()`. Per-Code pools do
not exist in this configuration.

So scanning "the caller's pool" is not a thing in AOT. The v1 draft of this code
walked global_object_pool() and was closer to right; narrowing it to the caller's
own Code was the regression.

AND THE STATE MACHINE EARNED ITS KEEP AGAIN. NULL_POOL is reported as its own
cause. With counters alone this would have read "0 pooled Functions, 0 matching" --
an identity mismatch, the conclusion under test, from a null dereference guard.
Three runs now (28, 29) have been saved from that by refusing to classify.

THE REAL PROBLEM WITH THE GLOBAL POOL, stated before anyone builds it: a global
scan can only answer "is the patched Function in the pool at all", NOT "is it the
entry THIS call site loads". The call site's pool INDEX is what identifies the
entry, and release 26's disassembly already gives it: pool offset 0xd4a8 at the
kUnchecked site. So the next measurement is not a scan -- it is to read the global
pool at that specific index and compare the Function there with function_ptr.

That is a different instrument from the one built, and it needs the caller's pool
INDEX, not its name.
